          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"persuasion\":\"PERSUADED\",\"support_scope\":\"FULL\",\"rationale\":\"The printed references support the entry’s compact pattern: east is the oriented front of the sanctuary, Eden lies eastward at humanity’s beginning, divine glory enters again through the eastern gate, and Christ’s coming is compared to motion from east to west. The Hebrew semantic association of qedem with east, what is before, and antiquity supplies a genuine lexical basis for the synthesis. The strongest common alternative—east as divine presence, paradise, light, and advent—is compatible with rather than contradictory to this core and is essentially a broader theological elaboration of the same cited pattern. Negative eastern motifs elsewhere show that the term is context-sensitive, but the entry does not expressly claim that its definition exhausts every biblical use.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"East is the scripturally oriented “front,” associated with beginnings and with divine or eschatological return.\",\"book_secondary_elaborations\":[\"Hebrew qedem links the senses “east,” “before,” and “ancient.”\",\"Eden was planted eastward.\",\"The sanctuary’s front entrance was its eastern gate.\",\"God’s glory returned through the eastern gate.\",\"Christ’s coming is depicted through east-to-west movement.\",\"The literal compass direction underlies the symbolic meaning.\",\"West is the directional opposite of east.\"],\"unsupported_glossary_assertions\":[],\"chapter_only_objections\":[\"The chapter’s inference from the east/front and west/behind word families to a final Babylon located in the far west is substantially more speculative than the glossary entry’s lexical claim.\",\"The chapter’s identification of the last Babylon with a particular western bloc and Jerusalem is not established by the glossary references.\",\"The chapter’s larger Exodus–Revelation typology, serpent-rod interpretation, and related prophetic equations require separate assessment and are not assertions of this entry.\"],\"strongest_counter_interpretation\":\"East has no single context-independent symbolism; in the cited positive texts it primarily signifies paradise, sacred access, divine glory, and the advent of Christ, while other passages associate eastward movement or the east wind with exile, rebellion, and judgment.\",\"counter_core_identification\":\"East is a context-sensitive direction whose dominant positive Christian symbolism is divine manifestation and advent rather than a universal symbol limited to beginnings and returns.\",\"counter_relation_to_book_core\":\"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\"material_core_denial\":\"The counter denies only that “beginnings and returns—the front” is an exhaustive or context-independent definition. The entry does not expressly make that exclusivity claim, and its selected pattern remains within the counter’s broader positive cluster.\",\"counter_case\":[\"Ezekiel 43 most directly concerns the return of divine glory, making divine presence an obvious theological emphasis.\",\"Matthew 24:27 is a simile for the conspicuous coming of the Son of Man, not a literal prediction that Christ travels along a compass route.\",\"Genesis 3:24; 4:16; 11:2; and 13:11 support a recognized negative motif of eastward alienation.\",\"East-wind texts associate the direction with judgment or affliction, demonstrating contextual variation.\",\"Historic Christian eastward orientation is normally explained through paradise, dawn, resurrection, and Christ’s return.\"],\"book_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It connects the lexical senses of qedem with the physical orientation of the sanctuary rather than merely collecting positive theological associations.\",\"It accounts directly for the eastern court gate in Exodus 27, which by itself does not explicitly mention light or Christological symbolism.\",\"It gives a concise progression from Edenic beginning through restored glory to eschatological coming.\",\"Its “front” language explains why the eastern entrance can function as the privileged approach to sacred space.\"],\"counter_explanatory_advantages\":[\"It accounts for the wider canon’s positive and negative uses without imposing one emotional or theological value on every occurrence.\",\"It aligns closely with established Christian liturgical explanations involving divine appearing, paradise, dawn, and resurrection.\",\"It distinguishes geographical, architectural, meteorological, and symbolic uses of east.\"],\"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\":[],\"comparative_winner\":\"BOOK\",\"decisive_sources\":[\"Genesis 2:8\",\"Exodus 27:13-16\",\"Ezekiel 43:1-4\",\"Ezekiel 46:1-3\",\"Matthew 24:27\",\"The Hebrew semantic range of qedem reflected in the entry and supporting chapter\"],\"unresolved_objections\":[\"“Christ’s coming moves from east to west” should be understood as shorthand for Matthew’s lightning simile, not as literal travel.\",\"The headline’s term “beginnings” is supported chiefly through Eden and the antiquity sense of qedem rather than through multiple explicit beginning texts.\",\"The entry is selective and should not be treated as excluding well-attested eastern motifs of exile, judgment, foreign nations, or idolatry.\"],\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"
        }
      ],
